    <entry>
        <title type="html"><![CDATA[Document that the Japanese calendar is currently broken in browsers for dates before 1868-10-23]]></title>
        <id>https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/41420</id>
        <link href="https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/41420"/>
        <updated>2025-10-08T16:28:12.000Z</updated>
        <content type="html"><![CDATA[<img src="https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/478856?u=efbc4deb51e7378936b0bca66d7da6a08279e86d&v=4" width="64" height="64" alt=""/><br/><h3 dir="auto">Description</h3>
<p dir="auto">Adds a note that the Japanese calendar doesn't actually at present work correctly in browser for dates before 1868-10-23.</p>
<h3 dir="auto">Motivation</h3>
<p dir="auto">Helps readers avoid triggering a cross-browser bug.</p>
<h3 dir="auto">Additional details</h3>
<p dir="auto">See <a href="https://unicode-org.atlassian.net/browse/CLDR-11375" rel="nofollow">https://unicode-org.atlassian.net/browse/CLDR-11375</a> (linked from the text of the PR) for the bug that causes all browsers to have incorrect data for the start of the Meiji era.</p>
<p dir="auto">Also, see <a href="https://tc39.es/proposal-intl-era-monthcode/" rel="nofollow">https://tc39.es/proposal-intl-era-monthcode/</a> , which requires browsers to use Gregorian eras for the Japanese calendar for dates before the Meiji era.</p>]]></content>
        <author>
            <name>hsivonen</name>
            <email>hsivonen@noreply.github.com</email>
            <uri>https://github.com/hsivonen</uri>
        </author>
        <published>2025-10-07T09:10:34.000Z</published>
    </entry>
    <entry>
        <title type="html"><![CDATA[Add note for anchor positioning fallbacks behaviour]]></title>
        <id>https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/41419</id>
        <link href="https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/41419"/>
        <updated>2025-10-07T16:55:23.000Z</updated>
        <content type="html"><![CDATA[<img src="https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/105991?u=9117b059ac229cd023e2188900da14bde6d83fa4&v=4" width="64" height="64" alt=""/><br/><h3 dir="auto">Description</h3>
<p dir="auto">Adding a note for position-try-fallbacks to explain that the element does not revert to its original position when available space is restored.</p>
<h3 dir="auto">Motivation</h3>
<p dir="auto">Developer's intuition for how anchor positioning fallbacks works does not match the specced behaviour, as demonstrated in <a href="https://mastodon.social/@Meyerweb/115106404124445073" rel="nofollow">this poll</a></p>
<p dir="auto">The spec is not expected to change - <a class="issue-link js-issue-link" data-error-text="Failed to load title" data-id="3367549498" data-permission-text="Title is private" data-url="https://github.com/w3c/csswg-drafts/issues/12682" data-hovercard-type="issue" data-hovercard-url="/w3c/csswg-drafts/issues/12682/hovercard" href="https://github.com/w3c/csswg-drafts/issues/12682">w3c/csswg-drafts#12682</a> - so a note to developers can help prevent the pitfall of not understanding why it's not going back to the starting position.</p>]]></content>
        <author>
            <name>djmarland</name>
            <email>djmarland@noreply.github.com</email>
            <uri>https://github.com/djmarland</uri>
        </author>
        <published>2025-10-07T09:01:57.000Z</published>
    </entry>
